[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Naming / linkage rules
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Public C API functions -> `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn <name>(...)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Use `extern "C" fn` (no `unsafe`) only when the function takes no raw pointers.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `include/sodium/private/quirks.h` **renames** many internal functions with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `_sodium_` prefix, e.g. `#define blake2b_init _sodium_blake2b_init`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Those are real exported linker symbols. For such functions the Rust item MUST
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   be `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn _sodium_blake2b_init(...)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (the FINAL linker name), and other Rust code calls it by that final name.
